{"qid":"Q28428","names":["Baekje"],"title":"Baekje","endonym":{"native":"백제","lang":"ko"},"capsule":"Baekje (백제) was one of the Three Kingdoms of Korea, traditionally founded in 18 BCE by Onjo, a son of Goguryeo's legendary founder, at Wiryeseong near present-day Seoul. At its height in the fourth century under King Geunchogo (r. 346–375), Baekje controlled most of the Korean peninsula's southwest and maintained a maritime network reaching Liaoxi and Japan, serving as the primary conduit for transmitting Chinese writing, Buddhism, and continental crafts and technology to the Japanese archipelago. A Silla-Tang alliance crushed Baekje's army under General Gyebaek at the Battle of Hwangsanbeol in 660 CE and captured the capital Sabi; a Japanese-backed restoration effort was decisively defeated at the Battle of Baekgang (663), and Baekje's territory was absorbed into Unified Silla.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Baekje's territorial core in the Korean southwest is well established archaeologically and textually, but exact boundaries against Goguryeo and Silla shifted frequently and are reconstructed with moderate confidence.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Baekje","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baekje"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Battle of Hwangsanbeol","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Hwangsanbeol"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Three Kingdoms of Korea","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Three_Kingdoms_of_Korea"}],"britannica":"place/Paekche","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Baekje","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Geunchogo of Baekje","nativeName":"近肖古王","roman":"Geunchogo-wang","start":-3